  "id": "COMPOUND:14824 - PROTEIN:6326",
  "verbalization": "Heparin is implicated in the regulation of Phosphodiesterase 6C through a series of molecular interactions involving SNU-387, a compound that serves as a target of multiple heparin-derived molecules. These molecules, including 3-Bromo-4-(2,3-Dibromo-4,5-Dihydroxybenzyl)-5-Methoxymethylpyrocatecol and 2-(hydroxymethyl)-3-[(E)-prop-1-enyl]phenol, all share the common target SNU-387 and ultimately lead to the inhibition of Phosphodiesterase 6C. The presence of multiple distinct pathways converging on SNU-387 suggests a potential role for heparin in modulating the activity of this enzyme, possibly through the activation of downstream signaling pathways. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the molecular interactions, where heparin may exert its effects by interacting with various compounds that target SNU-387 and subsequently influence Phosphodiesterase 6C. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between heparin and Phosphodiesterase 6C, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
